                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="604-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_hc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Hypercardioid" title="Hypercardioid" ></span>Microtech Gefell M 910</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Hypercardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The M 910 is a large-diaphragm FET condenser with an unusual shape, designed for uncolored reproduction of vocal and instrumental soloists.</p><p>It is equipped with a -10dB pad and a high-pass filter.  See also the M900, a cardioid version of the same microphone.</p><p>Gefell recommends a minimum working distance of 8-12 inches for vocal use and suggests further that at closer distances the proximity effect will emphasize the low frequencies.</p><p>The M910 ships in a wooden case, with a dark bronze finish (p/n 211141). Optional accessories include a foam windscreen (p/n W 90), pop screen (p/n PO 70), stand clamp (p/n MKV 8), elastic suspension mount (p/n EA90 for dark bronze, AT 8410a for &#8220;anthracite&#8221;), and more.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Microtech-Gefell/M-910">Microtech Gefell M 910</a> is also known as: M910.</p>
